Here's a side-by-side comparison of the two as backend languages:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([c1, m1])
c1 = Comparison("Backend: Python vs JavaScript (Node.js)", [
  {name:"Python", attributes:["Mature, readable syntax; great for beginners","Rich scientific/data ecosystem (pandas, NumPy, ML)","Slower CPU throughput; GIL limits parallel CPU work","Best for APIs over data/ML, scripting, automation","Frameworks: FastAPI, Django, Flask","pip/Poetry for packages"]},
  {name:"JavaScript (Node.js)", attributes:["Single language across frontend + backend","High concurrency I/O via non-blocking event loop","Node's single-threaded CPU tasks can block","Best for real-time, chat, webhooks, microservices","Frameworks: Express, NestJS, Fastify","npm/pnpm/yarn for packages"]}
])
m1 = Metric("Bottom line", "Pick based on team + use case, not hype")
:::

**Recommendation:** Choose **Python** if your backend is data-heavy, does ML/scientific work, or your team already writes Python. Choose **JavaScript/Node** if you want one language across the stack, high I/O concurrency (chat, streaming, webhooks), or ship quickly with a frontend team that already knows JS. Either is production-ready — the deciding factors are usually team skills and existing infrastructure, not raw performance.

